# NoOp — the effect that does nothing, for when doing nothing must be said.

# A pure body and a fully-discharged one leave the same empty residual row, so a `<*>` slot
# cannot tell them apart; performing `NoOp` states which one the author meant.
## An effect with no consequence, used to state "this body needs no effects" to a `<*>` slot.
## e.g. `comptime { eval = {} -> perform NoOpHandler in do … }` with `NoOp.noop {}` inside.
effect NoOp where {
  ## Do nothing, and be seen doing it.
  noop: {} -> {} <NoOp>
}

# `{}` is an implicit resume with unit: ~2.4x cheaper than `cont.resume { val = {} }` (no @TruffleBoundary).
## The only handler `NoOp` needs: it does nothing.
impl NoOp for NoOpHandler where {
  noop = {} -> {}
}

## Perform and handle `NoOp` in one call; satisfies a `<*>` slot like an inline `perform`.
pub let noop: {} -> {} <*> = {} -> perform NoOpHandler in NoOp.noop {}
